#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The executor of one machine of the pool: it hands the machine's slots to the requests queued in the pool repository.

usage: executor run --machine NAME    the loop; cron starts it every minute under flock, so there is one per machine
       executor _job RUNDIR           (internal) prepare the work tree on the host, then start the supervisor in the slot
       executor _sup RUNDIR           (internal) the supervisor: runs the command, enforces the timeout and a cancel

Everything shared is a JSON blob behind a ref of the pool repository (machines.git), like the heartbeats:
  refs/q/<id>       a request, written by flow: a job (project, commit, command, needs, priority, timeout) or a hold
  refs/run/<id>     written once by the executor that took the request (machine, slot, start, run directory). It is
                    created with "this ref must not exist", so of two executors that want the same request one wins
  refs/done/<id>    the conclusion (exit code, last line, seconds, the last 40 lines of the log, where the log is);
                    the same push deletes q and run. Swept 7 days after the end
  refs/cancel/<id>  asks the executor that runs <id> to end it; a request whose q ref is gone is cancelled as well
  refs/hb/<machine> the machine's heartbeat (beat); it carries the ids the machine's executor holds, so a run is alive
                    while its machine's heartbeat moves and lists it
A request that is not well formed, or that no slot of the pool fits for half an hour, gets an error conclusion from
whichever executor sees it first. A run whose machine has no heartbeat, no executor that holds it, or is no longer in
machines.json for ten minutes gets a lost conclusion (255); its executor's own conclusion, if it comes later, replaces
it. A claim that this executor gave up on and that the hub took after all is deleted, so the request is queued again.
On the machine:
  ~/curryflow/exec/<machine>/        state.json (what beat reports), executor.log, served.json, abandoned.json
  ~/curryflow/mirror/<project>.git   the machine's bare mirror of a project; only incremental fetches from its hub
  <slot dir>/<project>               the slot's work tree of the project: a clone of the mirror, one checkout per job,
                                     ignored files (.build) stay for incremental builds
  <slot dir>/.cf/<id>/               a job's run directory: job.json, log, exit.json, done.json
The slot dir is ~/<container> for a slot with an incus container (the container mounts it at the same path) and
~/curryflow/slots/<slot> otherwise. Git runs on the host, pinned to the slot's CPUs; the command runs in the slot:
under taskset in a systemd user scope with MemoryMax (no container), or through incus exec as the host user's uid and
gid (container). incus kills the process it started when its client goes away, so in a container the supervisor
forks off that process; the launcher keeps waiting while the supervisor's alive file is fresh.

A job's processes are run by the supervisor, a child subreaper, so every descendant stays below it. The supervisor
ends them (timeout, cancel file, or what is left when the command exits) by walking its own process tree and writes
exit.json last. The launcher creates launch.json itself, exclusively, so a run directory never gets two launchers.
The executor only reads files of the run directory, so it can be restarted while jobs run.
"""
